    {"id": 3, "claim": "The external stimulus that triggers this specific alignment and movement is magnetism.", "evidence": "Since the mechanism is defined by the physical interaction between ferromagnetic iron crystals and a magnetic field, the requisite external stimulus is a magnetic field. This distinguishes magnetotaxis from other taxis types.", "weight": 0.3, "depends_on": [2]},
    {"id": 4, "claim": "Alternative stimuli (oxygen, light, heat) do not trigger the iron-based magnetic guidance mechanism.", "evidence": "Oxygen triggers aerotaxis via chemoreceptors; light triggers phototaxis via photopigments; heat triggers thermotaxis via temperature-sensitive channels. None of these mechanisms rely on the magnetic alignment of iron crystals, although magnetotactic bacteria may use magnetism to optimize their position relative to oxygen gradients.", "weight": 0.1, "depends_on": [3]}
